 ```
-- **Serving.** `Bun.serve` binds right away (an unusable port prints the error and exits, as an uncaught error does in Bun) and returns a `Server` (`port`, `hostname`, `url`, `stop(closeActive?)`); requests are served once the script's top level (or `main`) has finished, until every server is stopped. `workers: n` (Tov only) forks `n` processes sharing the sockets, supervised: a worker that dies is replaced, and SIGTERM/SIGINT stop them all. Limits (seconds; unset or 0: none): `headerTimeout` (a request head from its first byte, or a new connection from its accept), `bodyTimeout` (a body going without a byte) — both answer 408 and close — and `idleTimeout` (a connection with no request in hand; a request being handled is never timed out, unlike Bun's); `maxConnections` (Tov) answers requests on connections past it per process with 503, and out of descriptors a connection is answered 503 at once from a spare descriptor instead of the loop spinning on the listener. `server.openConnections` (Tov) counts the process's open connections. `Bun.onStopSignal(fn)` (Tov) makes SIGTERM and SIGINT call `fn(name)` on the loop instead of ending the process (in every worker, re-armed after the fork; the supervisor forwards the signal, closes its copy of the listeners, and exits 0 once every worker has exited cleanly), so a program can `stop()` its servers, let the requests in hand finish and exit. `Bun.fdLimit()` (Tov) is the soft RLIMIT_NOFILE. `req.signal`, as in Bun, aborts (an `AbortError`) when the client goes away — its connection closes or resets, or it ends its side — before an async handler has answered, so the handler can stop the work it started for it (pass it to `fetch`). `server.requestIP(req)` is the address of the client that sent a request the server received (`{ address, family, port }`, family `"IPv4"` or `"IPv6"`), or `undefined` for a request the program made, as in Bun.
+- **Serving.** `Bun.serve` binds right away (an unusable port prints the error and exits, as an uncaught error does in Bun) and returns a `Server` (`port`, `hostname`, `url`, `stop(closeActive?)`); requests are served once the script's top level (or `main`) has finished, until every server is stopped. `workers: n` (Tov only) forks `n` processes sharing the sockets, supervised: a worker that dies (a trap, or SIGKILL from the OOM killer) is replaced while the others keep serving, after a pause that starts at 1 s and doubles up to 30 s while it keeps dying within 10 s of starting, and SIGTERM/SIGINT stop them all. Limits (seconds; unset or 0: none): `headerTimeout` (a request head from its first byte, or a new connection from its accept), `bodyTimeout` (a body going without a byte) — both answer 408 and close — and `idleTimeout` (a connection with no request in hand; a request being handled is never timed out, unlike Bun's); `maxConnections` (Tov) answers requests on connections past it per process with 503, and out of descriptors a connection is answered 503 at once from a spare descriptor instead of the loop spinning on the listener. `server.openConnections` (Tov) counts the process's open connections. `Bun.onStopSignal(fn)` (Tov) makes SIGTERM and SIGINT call `fn(name)` on the loop instead of ending the process (in every worker, re-armed after the fork; the supervisor forwards the signal, closes its copy of the listeners, and exits 0 once every worker has exited cleanly), so a program can `stop()` its servers, let the requests in hand finish and exit. `Bun.fdLimit()` (Tov) is the soft RLIMIT_NOFILE. `Bun.plainAlloc()` (Tov) says whether the runtime was compiled with `TV_PLAIN_ALLOC` (every object from malloc; the small-object free lists are not thread-safe). `req.signal`, as in Bun, aborts (an `AbortError`) when the client goes away — its connection closes or resets, or it ends its side — before an async handler has answered, so the handler can stop the work it started for it (pass it to `fetch`). `server.requestIP(req)` is the address of the client that sent a request the server received (`{ address, family, port }`, family `"IPv4"` or `"IPv6"`), or `undefined` for a request the program made, as in Bun.
 - **Routing** as in Bun: exact paths first, then `:param` and `/*` routes; a route is a `Response`, a handler, or an object of per-method handlers (`GET`, `POST`, ...; others fall through to `fetch`). `req.params` is typed from the route. Unmatched requests go to `fetch`, else `404`.
 - **Handlers** return a `Response`, or (`async`) a promise of one: their type is `(req, server) => Response | Promise<Response, Error> throws Error`. A synchronous response is written at once; an async one when its promise settles, and responses on a connection still go out in request order (pipelining works either way).
 - **Errors.** A handler that throws (or whose promise rejects) is logged to stderr and answered by `error(err)` if given, else `500 Something went wrong!`, like Bun in production.
